# Code Smells

Treat any of the following as a defect to fix before marking a task complete. Check every changed file and every function you touch.

---

## Needless Repetition

### SMELL-01 — Duplicated logic

Do not write the same logic in two or more places. If behaviour is repeated, extract it to a single shared function and call it from all sites. Duplication means two places to update and two ways to diverge.

### SMELL-02 — Repeated null or default guards after a normalisation boundary

Do not scatter `?? default`, `or []`, or existence checks across every consumer for a field that has already been normalised to a required value at an entry boundary. Normalise once; the type encodes the guarantee downstream.

```
// bad — guard repeated at three consumers after the boundary already set tags
countTags(item):   return (item.tags or []).length
renderTags(item):  return join(item.tags or [], ", ")
exportTags(item):  return csv(item.tags or [])

// good — normalise once; consumers trust the type
normalise(raw): return { ...raw, tags: raw.tags if raw.tags else [] }
countTags(item):   return item.tags.length
renderTags(item):  return join(item.tags, ", ")
exportTags(item):  return csv(item.tags)
```

### SMELL-03 — Redundant type alias

Do not create a second type alias that is structurally identical to one that already exists. If tightening one optional field on a draft type yields the same shape as the domain type, use the domain type directly; the alias is pure noise.

---

## Needless Complexity

### SMELL-04 — Useless intermediate variable

Do not add a variable that is set once, used once, with no transformation. Inline the expression.

```
// bad
key = item.id
render(key)

// good
render(item.id)
```

Exception: a name that disambiguates a genuinely complex or non-obvious expression is justified (high bar). A name that only repeats a property path is not.

### SMELL-05 — No-op spread or field assignment

Do not explicitly re-assign a property inside an object literal when the spread source already carries that property at the same value. The assignment is invisible and misleads reviewers.

```
// bad — id is already on item; this changes nothing
items.map(item => ({ ...item, id: item.id }))

// good
items.map(item => ({ ...item }))
```

Before keeping an override, verify: (1) the field's type cannot be `undefined` at this point, and (2) the mapping function actually changes the value. If both checks pass, the override is a no-op.

### SMELL-06 — Dead condition

Do not write conditions whose outcome is fixed by the surrounding type constraints. A branch that can never be entered, or one that is always entered, is dead code.

```
// bad — field is required; it can never be undefined here
if field != null and field == TARGET: ...

// good
if field == TARGET: ...
```

Before adding any condition, verify it is not already implied by the type of the variable or by a previous check in the same scope.

### SMELL-07 — Redundant structural assertion

Do not assert a structural constraint (collection length, field presence) in business logic when every path that produces the value already enforces that constraint. Trace all entry paths before adding such a check.

### SMELL-08 — `any` type

Do not use `any` (or its equivalent in your language). `any` opts out of type checking and hides bugs that the type system would otherwise catch. Use a specific type, `unknown` with a type guard, or infer from a schema.

### SMELL-09 — Type assertion without runtime check

Do not cast a value to a type (`as SomeType`, unsafe cast) without a runtime guard that validates the shape. A cast with no check provides no safety; it only silences the compiler.

```
// bad — no runtime check; hides invalid data
user = data as User

// good — runtime guard validates and narrows
user = parseUser(data)
if not user: raise Error("Invalid user")
```

---

## Opacity

### SMELL-10 — Unused imports, variables, or exports

Every import, variable, and exported symbol must be consumed somewhere reachable. Remove unused items immediately — they mislead static analysis and increase cognitive load.

### SMELL-11 — Parallel re-extraction of parent fields

Do not pull sub-fields of an object into sibling variables when those fields are already accessible on the object. Copies shadow the single source of truth and must be kept in sync manually.

```
// bad — tags and count duplicate fields already inside result
result, tags, count = compute()  // compute returns { result, tags, count }
// where tags and count are also fields on result

// good — return only result; read result.tags and result.count directly
result = compute()
use(result.tags, result.count)
```

### SMELL-12 — Redundant array alongside a map or record

Do not maintain a hand-built array that repeats entries already represented in an object, map, or record. Derive the list from the canonical structure instead.

```
// bad — allKeys duplicates config; must be kept in sync manually
config = { a: "a", b: "b" }
allKeys = [config.a, config.b]

// good — derive when you need an iterable
for value in config.values(): ...
```

---

## Rigidity and Fragility

### SMELL-13 — Fabricated fallback on a required field

Do not invent a fallback value for a field that is required by its type. A silent default masks a data error and makes the defect invisible.

```
// bad — id is required; inventing one hides the missing data
id = record.id or generateId()

// good — surface the breach explicitly
if not record.id: raise Error("Record missing required id: " + stringify(record))
```

This also applies when a field is optional in a stored type but has been normalised to required at a boundary. Do not re-apply a default guard downstream of that boundary.

### SMELL-14 — Client-local time for security or deadline logic

Do not use the client's local clock for deadline, expiry, or access-control decisions. Users can bypass checks by changing their device time. Base comparisons on server-provided time.

```
// bad — bypassable via device clock change
isPastDeadline = now() > deadline

// good — anchored to server time
serverNow = serverTime + (localNow - syncTimestamp)
isPastDeadline = serverNow > deadline
```

### SMELL-15 — Silent fix hiding a server-owned integrity breach

Do not silently repair data on the client when the contract says the server must supply it. A silent fix makes the UI appear to work while bad data persists in storage and other consumers are unaffected.

Prefer: visible failure, observable logging, or a fix at the authoritative layer. A client shim is only justified as a time-bounded bridge with a tracked removal plan.

---

## Immobility

### SMELL-16 — Duplicated field at parent and child level

Do not store a field inside a child object when the same field already exists at the parent level of the same entity. Both copies hold the same value at creation but are independent — they can silently diverge.

```
// bad — id already on Node; copying into data creates a second source of truth
node = { id: "n1", data: { id: "n1", label: "A" } }

// good — data holds only what is unique to it
node = { id: "n1", data: { label: "A" } }
```
